WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.046 --> 00:00:09.068
FAYE MOSS: Hello, everyone! Welcome back to Superstars in History —the talk show featuring famous people from the

2
00:00:09.069 --> 00:00:18.256
past. I'm your host, Faye Moss, and today's guest is an American abolitionist whose courage will amaze you!

3
00:00:18.257 --> 00:00:27.131
After escaping slavery herself, she bravely led many other slaves to freedom along the Underground Railroad. Please

4
00:00:27.132 --> 00:00:46.570
join me in welcoming Harriet Tubman! HARRIET

5
00:00:46.571 --> 00:00:48.916
TUBMAN: Thank you,

6
00:00:48.917 --> 00:00:54.561
Faye! FAYE MOSS: I know you were born around 1820, and your parents named

7
00:00:54.562 --> 00:01:00.820
you Araminta. They were slaves on plantations in Maryland—does that mean you were born into

8
00:01:00.821 --> 00:01:04.037
slavery? HARRIET TUBMAN: Yes, Faye, I was

9
00:01:04.038 --> 00:01:10.329
born a slave. Back then the United States had slave states in the South and free states in

10
00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:16.149
the North. As slaves, my parents worked day and night in their masters' houses and

11
00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:21.918
fields. I mostly played outdoors and looked after younger children—until around age

12
00:01:21.919 --> 00:01:25.258
six. FAYE MOSS: What happened then? Did you go

13
00:01:25.259 --> 00:01:36.026
to school? HARRIET TUBMAN: Oh no! Slaves rarely went to school—in fact, I never even learned to read or write. Instead my master began hiring me

14
00:01:36.027 --> 00:01:40.634
out to work. For the next few years, I was a weaver's helper, maid,

15
00:01:40.635 --> 00:01:47.783
and caretaker. One mistress whipped me if her baby cried too much—these whippings left

16
00:01:47.784 --> 00:01:53.780
terrible scars. FAYE MOSS: How awful! And that wasn't even your worst childhood

17
00:01:53.781 --> 00:02:01.420
injury, was it? HARRIET TUBMAN: No, I was about 13 when I entered a store where a slave was

18
00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:05.663
fleeing from his overseer. The overseer picked up a metal weight and flung it

19
00:02:05.664 --> 00:02:10.814
at the slave. But it hit me instead, gashing my forehead and

20
00:02:10.815 --> 00:02:15.752
knocking me unconscious. For the rest of my life, I suffered from headaches

21
00:02:15.753 --> 00:02:21.709
and seizures. Yet the injury had a bright side—I started having

22
00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:24.863
powerful visions. I believed these were messages from

23
00:02:24.864 --> 00:02:29.057
God. FAYE MOSS: Were any of your visions about

24
00:02:29.058 --> 00:02:32.659
escaping from slavery? HARRIET TUBMAN: Oh yes! I dreamed about

25
00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:37.581
freedom all the time. As a child I heard plenty of stories about slaves escaping

26
00:02:37.582 --> 00:02:45.535
to the North. There were rumors about an "underground railroad," a secret network of safe houses where runaway slaves could

27
00:02:45.536 --> 00:02:52.249
get help. FAYE MOSS: During childhood you learned some valuable skills from your father. What

28
00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:57.891
did he teach you? HARRIET TUBMAN: Well, I often worked outdoors with my daddy,

29
00:02:57.892 --> 00:03:07.631
chopping wood and plowing fields. He taught me lots about nature. He showed me which plants are edible, and how the North Star always shines from above,

30
00:03:07.632 --> 00:03:15.566
pointing the way north. He also taught me how to move silently through the woods—a skill that was mighty

31
00:03:15.567 --> 00:03:21.928
useful later on. FAYE MOSS: In 1844 you married John Tubman, who was

32
00:03:21.929 --> 00:03:28.648
a free man, right? HARRIET TUBMAN: That's right. I married John and changed my first name

33
00:03:28.649 --> 00:03:38.211
to Harriet, my mother's name. By now times were growing hard on my master's plantation. I knew I'd be sold soon, and I wanted to be

34
00:03:38.212 --> 00:03:48.005
free like my husband. One day a white woman stopped to ask about my scars. She told me where she lived and promised to help me if I

35
00:03:48.006 --> 00:03:52.068
ever needed help. FAYE MOSS: I think that

36
00:03:52.069 --> 00:03:58.247
day came in 1849? HARRIET TUBMAN: Yes, in 1849 I ran

37
00:03:58.248 --> 00:04:04.418
away with two of my brothers. But soon after we started out, they got cold

38
00:04:04.419 --> 00:04:10.234
feet—worrying it was too dangerous. We turned back, but that December I escaped again, this

39
00:04:10.235 --> 00:04:18.906
time on my own. I found the white woman's house, and she told me about Underground Railroad conductors, folks who'd help me along

40
00:04:18.907 --> 00:04:24.728
the journey northward. I set out, traveling at night and relying on the North Star to

41
00:04:24.729 --> 00:04:30.225
guide me. When I finally reached the free state of Pennsylvania, I felt

42
00:04:30.226 --> 00:04:38.619
different. There was such a glory over everything; the sun came like gold through the trees . . . like heaven!

43
00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:42.459
I had to look at my hands to see if I was the same person.

44
00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:51.768
FAYE MOSS: You walked nearly a hundred miles by yourself in the cold and dark to be free! What happened

45
00:04:51.769 --> 00:04:59.170
next? HARRIET TUBMAN: In Philadelphia I worked odd jobs, but I often felt lonely, like a stranger in a

46
00:04:59.171 --> 00:05:03.067
strange land. I had dreams about my family and bringing them up

47
00:05:03.068 --> 00:05:11.356
North. Eventually I became a conductor on the Railroad, and in 1850, I led my sister's family out of Maryland.

48
00:05:11.357 --> 00:05:19.819
The next year I went back, and I kept returning, probably close to 13 times. On

49
00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:28.562
one trip I went to fetch my husband, and can you believe it—that man had married another woman! He ended

50
00:05:28.563 --> 00:05:36.518
up staying in Maryland. In 1857 on another trip, I conducted my parents up north. FAYE MOSS: Did

51
00:05:36.519 --> 00:05:41.986
you ever encounter slave catchers—people who returned slaves to their owners? HARRIET

52
00:05:41.987 --> 00:05:45.957
TUBMAN: I had some close calls, but I never got caught.

53
00:05:45.958 --> 00:05:54.682
I believed God was watching over me, and I had ways of protecting myself, like traveling at night and in the wintertime. Oh,

54
00:05:54.683 --> 00:05:58.980
and I carried a revolver, just in case. FAYE

55
00:05:58.981 --> 00:06:05.647
MOSS: Many people have compared you to Moses from the Bible, who led slaves out of Egypt.

56
00:06:05.648 --> 00:06:19.263
HARRIET TUBMAN: Yes, people even called me Moses, and stories about Moses reached the ears of slave catchers, but few guessed that Moses was

57
00:06:19.264 --> 00:06:25.914
female! FAYE MOSS: You stopped conducting around 1860, but you continued to do some

58
00:06:25.915 --> 00:06:34.282
dangerous work. When the Civil War broke out in 1861, you worked for the North as a nurse—and a

59
00:06:34.283 --> 00:06:41.849
spy! HARRIET TUBMAN: The Civil War was partly a fight about slavery between the Union in the North and the Confederates in

60
00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:47.643
the South. The North wanted to abolish it, while the South wanted to preserve the right to own

61
00:06:47.644 --> 00:06:54.242
slaves. So of course, I did my part. When Union soldiers got injured, I nursed them.

62
00:06:54.243 --> 00:07:01.156
And since I knew how to move around undetected, I also spied on Confederate soldiers for the Union. In

63
00:07:01.157 --> 00:07:07.334
1863 I became the first woman ever to lead an armed attack in the U.S. Our army

64
00:07:07.335 --> 00:07:14.572
of black soldiers freed about 750 slaves from three plantations! FAYE MOSS:

65
00:07:14.573 --> 00:07:24.807
That's unbelievable! In 1865 the North won the war, and slavery was abolished. What did you do next? HARRIET

66
00:07:24.808 --> 00:07:30.340
TUBMAN: I got married again and joined the suffrage movement to fight for women's voting rights.

67
00:07:30.341 --> 00:07:38.261
I also opened a retirement home for former slaves that had an infirmary that provided free healthcare. FAYE

68
00:07:38.262 --> 00:07:45.549
MOSS: The next time I need to summon up some courage, Harriet, I'm definitely going to think of you.

69
00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:50.780
You're amazing! We'll let you return to history now!

70
00:07:50.781 --> 00:07:56.238
HARRIET TUBMAN: Thank you kindly,

71
00:07:56.239 --> 00:08:05.891
Faye. FAYE MOSS: For about ten years, from 1850-1860, Harriet Tubman led more than seventy

72
00:08:05.892 --> 00:08:13.856
slaves to freedom. She risked her life as well as her own freedom, and she never got caught or lost

73
00:08:13.857 --> 00:08:23.037
a passenger. Harriet Tubman died in 1913. Her bravery inspired many others to fight for civil

74
00:08:23.038 --> 00:08:33.634
rights. Thank you for joining us today. I'll be back soon to interview another superstar from the past!

